WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.160
X-Men: The Animated Series dazzled '90s viewers&nbsp;
with action, drama, and real-world storytelling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.040
So sophisticated is this cartoon, in fact,&nbsp;
that a whole lot of it goes over kids' heads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.200
We're here to explore those details&nbsp;
aimed straight at X-Men's older audience.&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.560
Disenfranchised people have long seen themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.080
in the X-Men, and the creators behind the animated&nbsp;
series knew how important it was to respect that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.560
As writer Julia Lewald told Vulture,
"[Fox was] very conscious of making the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:34.080
show inclusive without being intrusive about it."
The show's interest in diversity extended beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.120
the main cast, as well. Bishop, a Black mutant&nbsp;
from the future, was one one of the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.960
most memorable guest characters. Racism is also&nbsp;
handled head-on. In the episode One Man’s Worth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.360
the team is forced to deal with a racist who&nbsp;
confronts Storm and Wolverine when he sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.240
them holding hands. The series also doesn't&nbsp;
shy away from powerful women, allowing Rogue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.520
Storm, Jean Grey, and others to dazzle on screen.&nbsp;
This was accomplished in part by deliberately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.440
hiring more female writers.
"Forget it lady!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.280
Scott's my date!"
X-Men is even relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.000
progressive where gender identity is concerned.&nbsp;
Consider the actions of shapeshifters such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.160
Morph and Mystique, who often change&nbsp;
gender to flirt with the opposite sex.&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.720
It might seem smart to keep politics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.400
out of a Saturday morning cartoon, but there's&nbsp;
no way to completely eliminate the political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.680
nature of the X-Men. One of the series' main&nbsp;
antagonists in the first few episodes is Senator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.840
Robert Kelly, who declares his intentions&nbsp;
to run for president. Kelly is playing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.640
his highly xenophobic base, which means&nbsp;
that his policies are heavily anti-mutant.&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.320
Later on, Professor X takes center&nbsp;
stage when he meets with politicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.600
and even goes to Washington to speak on behalf&nbsp;
of mutants everywhere. Russian politics come up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.320
in an episode in which the Russian X-Man&nbsp;
Colossus' past is explored, and Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.120
serial killer Omega Red attempts to overthrow&nbsp;
the government. Not even Canada looks clean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.760
once Wolverine's past working for the Canadian&nbsp;
government is covered in a searing episode.&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.080
X-Men also explores intra-mutant&nbsp;
politics, like those of the Morlocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.120
a band of mutants who dwell underground. Storm&nbsp;
ultimately challenges Callisto, their leader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.840
in an effort to save the team. Her victory makes&nbsp;
Storm the technical leader of the sewer-dwellers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.640
though she doesn't stick around to abuse that&nbsp;
power and later relinquishes it back to Callisto.&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.280
Superhero comics usually follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.560
individuals portrayed as being at peak physical&nbsp;
condition, who are poured into tight spandex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.440
The X-Men and many of their foes are no&nbsp;
exception, and their cartoon gets positively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.480
risqué at times. Consider Rogue, who is one of&nbsp;
the more flirtatious characters in the show.&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.000
She deals with the fact that her&nbsp;
mutant power means that she can't touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.360
anyone by delivering flirty lines. When&nbsp;
Rogue first sees Colossus, she muses:&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.920
"Now that is a shame, locking up a big&nbsp;
good looking hunk of mutant like that."&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.600
Rogue's voice actress, Lenore Zann, has said&nbsp;
that creators were, quote, "asking for a sexy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.920
husky female voice with a Southern accent."
Clearly, they got what they asked for.&nbsp;

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.560
Flirty interactions between unexpected&nbsp;
characters often make the most impact in X-Men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.720
Sabretooth asks Wolverine if he wants to&nbsp;
"kiss and make up," while in the same episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:22.320
Rogue flirts with Cyclops as she gives him CPR:
"Come on, pretty boy, make a girl feel welcome."&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.320
She follows this up once their powers&nbsp;
return to normal by telling Cyclops that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.400
they’ll have to try it again sometime.
Things get even more overt when Cyclops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.280
is kidnapped by the Morlocks for the purpose of&nbsp;
giving Callisto an heir. Kids might not pick up on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.560
these sly innuendos, but adults definitely will.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.360
Slavery infests some of the darkest parts of&nbsp;
human history, and depicting it, especially in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.080
a Saturday morning cartoon, can leave quite an&nbsp;
impression. In a season one arc, several members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:55.920
of the X-Men are sent to investigate the tropical&nbsp;
vacation spot and mutant utopia known as Genosha.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.320
But everything about the island is a lie.
The heroes are soon captured and thrown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.560
into cells with other mutants, who are&nbsp;
kept alive and used as slave labor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.040
Each of them is forced to wear a collar that&nbsp;
dampens their powers, except when their powers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.720
necessary to do manual labor. It makes for brutal&nbsp;
viewing, especially for a children's cartoon.&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.520
Other forms of extreme bigotry are examined within&nbsp;
the series. When exploring Magneto's past, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.800
Holocaust is alluded to. Mutants are rounded up&nbsp;
and imprisoned, especially in episodes exploring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.400
the future. Senator Kelly even talks about putting&nbsp;
them in internment camps before he is saved by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.920
X-Men and turned into an ally. Mutants always seem&nbsp;
one step away from being banished, segregated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.080
or losing all of their rights.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.560
The X-Men deal with hatred and persecution&nbsp;
constantly. It's a theme that is explored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.560
throughout the majority of their comics,&nbsp;
and the cartoon captures it excellently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.760
Mutants are hated, hunted, and attacked. Their&nbsp;
most active human enemies are soon given a name:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.160
The Friends of Humanity. It isn't hard to&nbsp;
see that this collective is meant to mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.200
modern-day neo-Nazi groups, down to their&nbsp;
symbolism, rallies, and mob-like mentality.&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.480
The best example of this comes in&nbsp;
the episode Beauty and the Beast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.000
Beast and a colleague have developed a&nbsp;
treatment to restore a young woman's sight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.480
but her father hates Beast and wants him away&nbsp;
from his daughter. It's an emotional story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.560
as Beast is in love with the young woman,&nbsp;
who is later kidnapped by the hate group.&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.960
It is also an episode in which the audience&nbsp;
hears the word "bigot," as the show draws a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:25.920
direct parallel between the plight of mutants&nbsp;
and persecuted groups in the real world.&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.320
The AIDS epidemic was a major part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.520
'80s and '90s, one which led to the stigmatization&nbsp;
of the gay community. In addition to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.240
associated with other marginalized groups, the&nbsp;
X-Men are often seen as a reflection of the gay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.520
community as well. So it isn't a surprise that&nbsp;
an arc about a deadly virus sweeping through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.640
the mutant community has drawn some comparisons to&nbsp;
AIDS. Granted, the Legacy Virus storyline involves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:53.840
some outlandish superhero storytelling, what with&nbsp;
its time travel and the whole plague being a plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:59.120
by the villain Apocalypse. But there are still&nbsp;
some poignant moments, and the comparison holds.&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.600
Much in the same way that victims of the AIDS&nbsp;
epidemic were treated with fear and hostility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:08.480
by the mainstream, rather than the care and&nbsp;
compassion they deserved, the manufactured Legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:13.920
Virus is used to frame mutants in a bad light&nbsp;
and generate more hatred toward them. Anti-mutant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:18.480
bigots claim mutants infect humans with the&nbsp;
virus. Graydon Creed, leader of the F.O.H.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.440
accidentally infects himself with it, before&nbsp;
blaming his infection on the mutant Bishop.&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.320
In the future that Bishop is trying&nbsp;
to prevent, many mutants are shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:31.360
to be locked up in quarantine out of societal&nbsp;
fear, and even more have died from the Legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:35.600
Virus. Though the X-Men do eventually&nbsp;
save the day, the image of stigmatized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.520
suffering mutants lingers.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.200
Though the character spends the majority&nbsp;
of the first season in jail, Beast is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.360
in fact, lucky to be there, since he was&nbsp;
almost not included in the show at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:50.320
George Buza's performance and the writers' passion&nbsp;
for the character made sure Beast became a bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:55.600
part of season two. Audiences see Beast's big&nbsp;
brain at work all the time, and are regaled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.800
with his many musings, philosophical answers,&nbsp;
and overly verbose intellectual statements.&nbsp;

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:04.880
Younger viewers are most likely confused by this&nbsp;
blue-haired genius, as when, in one episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:09.200
he discusses a teammate's "nom de guerre."&nbsp;
Adults, however, might get a good chuckle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:14.640
out of Beast's advanced vocabulary. Beast is&nbsp;
often seen reading, especially while in prison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:19.680
It pays off: He readily cites the works of&nbsp;
Shakespeare, Lord Tennyson, and John Donne.&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.560
"Though I am always in haste, I am&nbsp;
never in a hurry. John Wesslian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:31.280
In mere moments the files will be no more."
Beast isn't the only one who gets to deploy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.400
references that most kids won't grasp, however.&nbsp;
Morph gets some stolen Robert Frost lines in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.360
Wolverine quotes Dirty Harry and The Shining&nbsp;
to terrifying effect. Rogue makes an obscure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:46.240
golf reference. Then there are the large number of&nbsp;
scientific terms used throughout the series, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:51.600
when the team learns about temporal displacement&nbsp;
and Darwinism. You certainly can’t accuse X-Men of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.000
lacking in educational content!

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.640
Being a member of the X-Men isn't easy. The&nbsp;
fighting is constant, and often leads to death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:02.640
Viewers are exposed to this early on in the first&nbsp;
story arc when Morph is thought to have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.760
killed. Later on, the Dark Phoenix storyline deals&nbsp;
a devastating blow to the X-Men with Jean Grey's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:12.960
apparent death. There is also the series finale,&nbsp;
which sees the team deal with the loss of Charles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.720
Xavier, who narrowly avoids death but becomes&nbsp;
lost to his mutant family, perhaps forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.520
While the deaths in the show often end up being&nbsp;
more temporary than they first seem, the grief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.920
the characters feel at the time is genuine.
The show also explores several alternate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:32.240
worlds that force viewers to confront the&nbsp;
deaths of beloved characters. The Days of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:37.520
Future Past episodes follow Wolverine in the&nbsp;
future, condemned to walk past the graves of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.880
all his fallen friends. Another world features a&nbsp;
married Wolverine and Storm, who are recruited to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:47.680
help set time right again. Tragically, doing&nbsp;
this means that their lives, and their love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.800
are erased. The tears they shed when they&nbsp;
realize that they have succeeded are deeply sad.&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:56.640
The most unique instance of loss on the&nbsp;
show comes when the mansion is destroyed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:02.400
Wolverine reminisces out loud about how it is one&nbsp;
of the few homes he's ever known. Seeing someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.520
so stoic lament a building is a moving moment.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:11.440
The X-Men have a lot of baggage, and relationship&nbsp;
drama is constant. Classic love triangles abound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:17.360
most prominently between Cyclops, Jean Grey, and&nbsp;
Wolverine, as well as unrequited love and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:22.400
divorce. Several mutants are disowned by their&nbsp;
parents. The Proteus episodes reveal that one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:27.040
of the world's most powerful mutants in the&nbsp;
world just wants acceptance from his father.&nbsp;

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:30.800
Throw in several interrupted marriages,&nbsp;
surprise announcements of parenthood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:35.760
and a couple of shapeshifters, and things get&nbsp;
crazy. Morph pretending to be Scott and Jean's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:41.200
priest to ensure their marriage's illegitimacy&nbsp;
is perhaps the peak of this pettiness and drama.&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:46.880
Frankly, X-Men feels like a daytime soap opera in&nbsp;
many ways. But these ultra-emotional moments also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:52.240
make the characters feel very, dare we say…human.
The series' writers wanted to showcase the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.720
characters battling their own inner demons&nbsp;
as well as their physical enemies. Viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.760
are the winners here, because this exploration&nbsp;
ultimately gives great depth to the storytelling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:06.960
Plus, as there isn't a lot of media for kids&nbsp;
that discuss things like death, divorce, and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:12.160
to deal with strong emotions. X-Men is downright&nbsp;
helpful to little ones seeking guidance. The show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:17.040
knows kids aren't stupid, even if they don't grasp&nbsp;
every single detail the way their parents do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:22.160
Incorporating so many dramatic elements might make&nbsp;
the show soapy, but it also makes it important.&nbsp;

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:23.680
"Ain't that enough?"

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:28.880
Religion is very much a part of X-Men. Fictional&nbsp;
religions exist, like those who worship Garokk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:34.880
in the Savage Lands. Moreover, Storm herself is&nbsp;
sometimes referred to as a goddess. Christianity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:39.920
is also present on the show, explored in episodes&nbsp;
like Descent, which discusses the evolution of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:45.120
mutants, and episodes featuring Nightcrawler,&nbsp;
whose Catholicism is a big part of his character.&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:50.960
Nightcrawler has an unsettling appearance. His&nbsp;
blue skin, yellow eyes, and prehensile tail give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:56.400
him the appearance of a demon. He even smells&nbsp;
of sulfur when using his teleportation powers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:59.600
It's no surprise then, that a mob&nbsp;
comes to destroy Nightcrawler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:05.160
insisting that God is with them on their mission.
An episode, appropriately enough titled Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.920
Crawler, explores having faith, losing it, and&nbsp;
renewing one's relationship with the divine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:13.280
It also discusses the question of why&nbsp;
God would create mutants in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:18.400
place. In an unexpected moment toward the end,&nbsp;
Nightcrawler gives Wolverine a Bible, with several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:24.080
marked passages. The episode ends on a scene of&nbsp;
Wolverine reading passages out loud in a church.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:29.120
This is particularly striking, given Wolverine's&nbsp;
cynicism. But somehow, against all odds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:33.360
Nightcrawler's kindness has reached him.
Nightcrawler speaks often about forgiveness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:38.000
and compassion, even for those in his family&nbsp;
who did horrible things to him. He always tries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:43.280
to live by God's words, and X-Men is interested&nbsp;
in exploring what that means, with a depth that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.360
adults are likely to appreciate.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:50.400
X-Men The Animated Series is full of violence. But&nbsp;
as is the case with many cartoons, few of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:55.760
kicks and punches can actually do any lasting&nbsp;
damage, or else things might get bloody. How do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.760 --> 00:12:01.440
you inject a sense of danger into action scenes,&nbsp;
then? The inclusion of Sentinels and other robots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:07.440
as primary antagonists helps tremendously with&nbsp;
this dilemma. These giant menaces are a godsend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:13.360
for simulated violence. As Julia Lewald remarked:
"If action's going to be big, it's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:18.240
involve Sentinels getting bits torn off and thrown&nbsp;
about. You couldn't do that with living things."&nbsp;

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:22.640
Ripping cybernetic parts off of a human, as&nbsp;
happens with Donald Pierce during the fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:27.600
with the Hellfire Club, is also allowed, but&nbsp;
skirts the line. These workarounds allow fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:31.840
to see Wolverine and others decapitate,&nbsp;
dismember, and eviscerate their enemies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:36.320
using their powers to their fullest extent.
It's not just the physical cost of violence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:40.960
skirts a mature line, either. Many characters&nbsp;
also go out of their way to hurt innocents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:46.320
or are obsessed with being cruel. Magneto&nbsp;
swears revenge on quite a few people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:51.440
Archangel dedicates his wealth to tracking down&nbsp;
and destroying Apocalypse in an episode blatantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:56.800
titled Obsession. Violence is ingrained in the&nbsp;
lives of these characters, and in the show itself.&nbsp;

